      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Automated Contact Pruning Runbook: Retaining Tier Thresholds with Webhooks &amp; Workflows
      </h2>
      <p>
        If your organization is currently locked into an active HubSpot annual contract, implement an aggressive automated pruning policy to prevent your database from accidentally triggering the next 5,000-contact pricing tier:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>90-Day Inactivity Downgrade Workflow</strong>: Create a HubSpot automated workflow triggered when <code>Last Marketing Email Open Date is more than 90 days ago</code> AND <code>Form Submission Date is more than 90 days ago</code>. Action: Set <code>Marketing Contact Status = Non-marketing contact</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Immediate Hard Bounce Quarantine</strong>: Configure a webhook to immediately transition any email address returning an SMTP 5xx permanent delivery failure to non-marketing status, ensuring bounced addresses never consume billable headroom.</li>
        <li><strong>Pre-Billing Day Audit</strong>: Schedule a reminder 3 days prior to your monthly billing snapshot date to execute an active contact purge, exporting and archiving cold contacts to your internal data warehouse.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Production ETL Migration Pipeline: Extracting HubSpot Contacts to PostgreSQL / Twenty CRM
      </h2>
      <p>
        Use the Python migration script below to paginate through HubSpot's REST API (v3), extract all contact records along with custom properties, filter out unengaged leads, and generate normalized JSON payloads ready for insertion into Twenty CRM or PostgreSQL:
      </p>

      <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono text-amber-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
HubSpot to Twenty CRM / PostgreSQL Automated ETL Extractor
Paginates through HubSpot Contacts API v3 and exports normalized lead payloads.
"""

import os
import sys
import json
import urllib.request
import urllib.parse

HUBSPOT_API_KEY = os.environ.get("HUBSPOT_ACCESS_TOKEN", "pat-na1-example-token")

def extract_hubspot_contacts(limit: int = 100):
    base_url = "https://api.hubapi.com/crm/v3/objects/contacts"
    properties = ["firstname", "lastname", "email", "company", "lifecyclestage", "hs_marketable_status"]
    
    extracted_records = []
    after_cursor = None
    
    while True:
        query_params = {
            "limit": limit,
            "properties": ",".join(properties),
            "archived": "false"
        }
        if after_cursor:
            query_params["after"] = after_cursor
            
        url = f"{base_url}?{urllib.parse.urlencode(query_params)}"
        req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={
            "Authorization": f"Bearer {HUBSPOT_API_KEY}",
            "Content-Type": "application/json"
        })
        
        try:
            with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=15) as resp:
                data = json.loads(resp.read().decode('utf-8'))
                results = data.get("results", [])
                
                for contact in results:
                    props = contact.get("properties", {})
                    extracted_records.append({
                        "hubspot_id": contact.get("id"),
                        "first_name": props.get("firstname"),
                        "last_name": props.get("lastname"),
                        "email": props.get("email"),
                        "company": props.get("company"),
                        "stage": props.get("lifecyclestage"),
                        "is_marketable": props.get("hs_marketable_status") == "true"
                    })
                
                paging = data.get("paging", {})
                next_page = paging.get("next", {})
                after_cursor = next_page.get("after")
                
                print(f"Extracted {len(extracted_records)} contacts so far...", file=sys.stderr)
                if not after_cursor:
                    break
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"Extraction halted: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            break

    return extracted_records

if __name__ == "__main__":
    records = extract_hubspot_contacts()
    with open("hubspot_contacts_export.json", "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        json.dump(records, f, indent=2)
    print(f"Successfully exported {len(records)} contacts to hubspot_contacts_export.json")
</code></pre>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Deliverability &amp; IP Infrastructure: HubSpot Shared Pools vs Dedicated Amazon SES Routing
      </h2>
      <p>
        One justification often cited by sales reps for HubSpot's premium pricing is deliverability. However, on standard Marketing Hub Professional tiers, your emails are sent through <strong>shared IP pools</strong> alongside thousands of other businesses, meaning another company's poor list hygiene can degrade your sender reputation.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
